    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>Declared illegitimate at two when her mother, Anne Boleyn, was executed, and imprisoned under her Catholic half-sister Mary, Elizabeth came to the throne in 1558 at the age of 25.</li><li>She established an English church with the monarch as supreme governor, never married or named an heir, kept Mary, Queen of Scots, captive for nineteen years before sanctioning her execution, and fought a long war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>.</li><li>Her 44 years on the throne gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> stability and a sense of national identity, though recent historians point to military failures, the tactics of her forces in Ireland and the economic troubles of her last years.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>Elizabeth translated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> and Greek authors throughout her life. A translation of the Annals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> in Lambeth Palace Library was confirmed as her own in 2019, after a detailed analysis of the handwriting and paper.</p></aside>
    </section>

